# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CACHING RULES FOR CLOUDFLARE PAGES.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING ANY OF IT.
#
# Measured, from the two hosts side by side:
#
#   arizonarabbitry.pages.dev/rabbits.js   max-age=0, must-revalidate
#   arizonarabbits.com/rabbits.js          max-age=14400
#
# Pages sends the right thing. The ZONE rewrites it. Cloudflare's
# Browser Cache TTL sits at its 4 hour default, and by its own rules
# it overrides the origin whenever the origin asks for LESS. So this
# file cannot win on its own, and the setting has to change too:
#
#   dash.cloudflare.com > arizonarabbits.com > Caching >
#   Configuration > Browser Cache TTL > "Respect Existing Headers"
#
# Until that is done, availability can be up to four hours stale in a
# returning visitor's browser. Two rabbits were marked reserved and
# the site kept showing them as available, which is how this was
# found.
#
# WHY THE SPLIT BELOW.
# A stale photograph is a mild problem: you see yesterday's picture of
# the right rabbit. Stale availability is a severe one: somebody asks
# about a rabbit that is already spoken for, and the site looks either
# careless or dishonest. So the two are cached completely differently.
#
# max-age=0 does NOT mean downloading it again every time. The ETag
# still stands, so an unchanged file answers 304 with no body. One
# small round trip, and availability is never wrong.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# THE ONE FILE THAT MUST NEVER BE STALE. Every price, status and
# countdown on the site is read from it.
/rabbits.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Carries what is available and what has been placed.
/litters.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Pictures, cached for a day rather than forever ON PURPOSE. Photos on
# this site DO get replaced under the same filename: every "Coming
# soon" frame becomes a real photograph eventually. Cache one of those
# for a year and the placeholder outlives the real picture.
# If you replace a photo and want it seen immediately, give the new
# file a new name and point rabbits.js at it. That is instant, and it
# costs nothing.
/photos/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/img/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
